Title:
UnwindRaven — Blueprint-driven synthetic call stack construction for Windows x64 threads

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
UnwindRaven is a Windows x64 offensive research framework that creates fully synthetic call stacks at thread startup so a new thread appears to originate from legitimate, trusted Windows frames when inspected by stack-walking tooling (e.g., debuggers/EDR using StackWalk64/RtlVirtualUnwind). It solves the portability problem in earlier call stack spoofing PoCs by making the call chain data-driven via a plaintext “blueprint” file rather than hardcoded, version-specific profiles. The framework derives accurate per-frame stack sizing by parsing PE exception directory metadata (UNWIND_INFO) at runtime and then builds a contiguous fake stack with correct return addresses and non-volatile register saves. It includes a companion harvester (BlueprintCallstack) that snapshots real threads in live processes and emits exported-frame blueprints, plus an integrated manual PE loader to run a payload DLL without LoadLibrary. A Vectored Exception Handler (VEH) handles the terminal unwind access violation and redirects execution to RtlExitUserThread for clean thread termination. This is primarily useful for red team tool developers and malware researchers studying stack-based EDR heuristics and evasion tradeoffs.

Technical Focus:
- Windows x64 stack spoofing / synthetic call stack construction
- PE exception directory parsing (RUNTIME_FUNCTION, UNWIND_INFO, UNWIND_CODE opcodes)
- Thread context manipulation (CONTEXT, SetThreadContext, suspended thread start)
- Vectored Exception Handling for controlled unwind/cleanup
- Manual PE mapping (sections, relocations, IAT resolution, memory protections)
- Call stack harvesting via StackWalk64 + DbgHelp/Symbol Server integration

Use Cases:
- Generate realistic-looking thread call stacks to test/evade stack-inspection detections
- Harvest “legit” call chains from target processes and replay them in a loader thread
- Research how EDRs correlate thread start, stack traces, and module provenance
- Build and evaluate manual-mapped payload execution under spoofed stacks
- Create reproducible lab experiments for unwind metadata and stack-walking behavior

Keywords:
Windows x64, call stack spoofing, synthetic stack, UNWIND_INFO, RUNTIME_FUNCTION, UNWIND_CODE, RtlVirtualUnwind, StackWalk64, DbgHelp, SymFromAddr, Vectored Exception Handler, SetThreadContext, CONTEXT, RtlExitUserThread, manual PE mapping, reflective loader, exception directory, .pdata, symbol server, EDR evasion